---
import { getCollection, render } from 'astro:content';
import lighthouseConfig from '../../lighthouserc.json';
import { scenarioIds } from '../bench/scenarios';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { readBudgets } from '../lib/budgets';
import { buildInfo } from '../lib/build-info';
import { identity } from '../lib/identity';

const decisions = (await getCollection('decisions')).sort((first, second) =>
  first.id.localeCompare(second.id),
);
const renderedDecisions = await Promise.all(
  decisions.map(async (decision) => ({ decision, Content: (await render(decision)).Content })),
);
const budgets = readBudgets(lighthouseConfig);
const repository = 'https://github.com/thomazzi98/thomazzi98.github.io';
const workflow = `${repository}/actions/workflows/ci.yml`;
---

<Base
  title="Colophon · Rafael Thomazzi"
  description="How this site is built and verified: the bench engine, the content model, the budgets the build enforces, and the decisions behind them."
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <h1>How this is made</h1>
    <p class="measure muted">
      A static site whose interactive parts are a small deterministic simulation engine. Everything
      below is verifiable from the <a href={repository}>repository</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="engine-title">
    <div class="section__head">
      <h2 id="engine-title">The bench engine</h2>
      <p class="kicker">{scenarioIds.length} scenarios · no runtime dependencies</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Each bench is a discrete-event simulation: a seeded random generator, a scheduler that
        releases events by virtual time and then by insertion order, and a reducer per scenario that
        turns an event into the next state and schedules what follows. Levers change future
        behaviour without touching events already queued. The renderer advances virtual time by a
        fixed amount every 120 ms beat and moves nothing between beats, so motion reads as a loom
        rather than an animation.
      </p>
      <p>
        The same engine runs at build time: every scenario has a scripted demonstration whose
        transcript is printed for readers without JavaScript and served as JSON next to the page.
        Under reduced motion nothing runs until Step is pressed, and every run is reproducible: the
        console prints the seed, and a <code>?seed=</code> parameter replays it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The drawings are laid out from the scenario graph at build, in both orientations, so a phone
        gets a vertical schematic rather than a shrunken one. The client code is a custom element
        that loads its scenario on demand and binds the simulation to markup that already exists,
        which keeps each page well under the script budget below.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="content-title">
    <div class="section__head">
      <h2 id="content-title">The content model</h2>
      <p class="kicker">Typed, referenced, checked at build</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Roles, projects, education and practices are content collections validated with schemas.
        Technologies are a registry that roles and projects reference by id; a reference to an
        unknown id fails the build, and so does a registry entry that nothing references. A practice
        must name the projects that back it. The resume, the llms.txt file, the stack page and the
        cloth on the About page are projections of those files, computed once.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="budgets-title">
    <div class="section__head">
      <h2 id="budgets-title">What the build refuses</h2>
      <p class="kicker">Enforced on every push</p>
    </div>
    <p class="measure muted">
      The pipeline type-checks, lints, checks formatting, rejects draft markers, runs the unit
      tests, builds, and checks every internal link. It then runs Playwright with axe-core against
      every route in the sitemap, once with JavaScript disabled, and asserts these Lighthouse
      budgets. A failure blocks the deploy. The latest run is on the{' '}
      <a href={workflow}>workflow page</a>.
    </p>
    <table class="stack-table budgets">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Audit</th>
          <th scope="col">Budget</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          budgets.map((budget) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{budget.audit}</th>
              <td>{budget.threshold}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="decisions-title">
    <div class="section__head">
      <h2 id="decisions-title">Decisions</h2>
      <p class="kicker">Recorded as they were made</p>
    </div>
    <div class="decisions">
      {
        renderedDecisions.map(({ decision, Content }) => (
          <article class="decision">
            <h3>{decision.data.title}</h3>
            <p class="kicker">
              {decision.data.status} · {decision.data.date.toISOString().slice(0, 10)}
            </p>
            <div class="prose">
              <Content />
            </div>
          </article>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="stamp-title">
    <div class="section__head">
      <h2 id="stamp-title">This build</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="mono">
      commit <a href={`${repository}/commit/${buildInfo.commit}`}>{buildInfo.commit}</a> ·{' '}
      {buildInfo.date} · type IBM Plex Sans and Mono, self-hosted · no analytics, no cookies, no third-party
      requests · {identity.name}, {identity.location}
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
